  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00034` — **SVG With Embedded Raster**, high severity, confidence 0.95, on `src/assets/partner-logo.svg`. The evidence, verbatim:\n\n> - \"2 `<image href=\"data:image/…;base64,…\">` occurrences\"\n> - \"embedded MIME types: image/jpeg, image/png\"\n> - \"SVG byte size: 592 bytes (includes the base64 overhead)\"\n> - \"re-author the raster region as vector paths, or split it out to its own asset\"\n\nSo: **2 embedded rasters**, MIME types **`image/jpeg`** and **`image/png`**.\n\n## What's actually in the file\n\n```\nsrc/assets/partner-logo.svg   200×80 viewBox, two 80×80 image slots\n  L3  <image x=\"0\"   … href=\"data:image/png;base64,…\">        ← the mark\n  L4  <image x=\"100\" … xlink:href=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,…\"> ← photographic\n```\n\nThere is no vector geometry at all — no `<path>`, `<rect>`, `<circle>`. The file is a 200×80 container whose entire visual payload is two base64 blobs. It's an SVG in extension only.\n\nTwo things the detector doesn't say that matter for the refactor:\n\n- The MIME split is a strong hint about intent. **PNG** (lossless, alpha) is what a design tool exports for flat logo geometry; **JPEG** (lossy, no alpha) is only ever chosen for continuous-tone photographic content. That's the line to cut along.\n- Line 4 uses `xlink:href` but the root `<svg>` declares no `xmlns:xlink`. That's an unrelated latent bug — it happens to render in browsers today because they special-case it, but it's invalid and stricter SVG toolchains will drop the element. Fix it in the same pass.\n\n## Remediation\n\n**1. Re-author as vector paths — the PNG slot (L3, `x=\"0\"`).**\nFlat logo geometry with alpha. Open the partner's source in Figma/Illustrator, convert strokes to outlines, flatten the layer, and export as SVG. The result becomes real `<path>` elements inside `partner-logo.svg`, sitting in the `0 0 80 80` region of the existing viewBox. If no vector source exists, ask the partner for their brand kit — every partner has one, and tracing a raster mark by hand produces a worse logo than asking.\n\n**2. Split out to a standalone raster — the JPEG slot (L4, `x=\"100\"`).**\nPhotographic content cannot be vectorized without becoming a different image; auto-tracers turn photos into thousands of polygons that render slower and weigh more than the original. This one leaves the SVG entirely, as `partner-logo-photo.webp` (plus a `.jpg` fallback if you support browsers that need it), sized to the dimensions it actually renders at.\n\n**3. Compose the two — and do not do it with an external `href` inside the SVG.**\nThe tempting move is to keep the `<image>` element and swap the data URI for `href=\"./partner-logo-photo.webp\"`. That silences the detector but breaks at runtime: when an SVG is loaded via `<img src>`, CSS `background-image`, or as a favicon, browsers block external resource fetches inside it, and the photo renders as a blank 80×80 gap. It only works when the SVG is inlined into the DOM.\n\nRecommended instead: make `partner-logo.svg` a pure 80×80 vector mark, and compose the pair in markup at the call sites.\n\n```html\n<span class=\"partner-logo\">\n  <img src=\"/assets/partner-logo.svg\"       alt=\"Partner\"        width=\"80\" height=\"80\">\n  <img src=\"/assets/partner-logo-photo.webp\" alt=\"\"  aria-hidden=\"true\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\">\n</span>\n```\n\nIf the two really must travel as one indivisible asset, the fallback is to keep the 200×80 SVG with an external `href` and guarantee every consumer inlines it — but that is a constraint you now have to enforce and document, which is why the split is the better default.\n\n## Files after the refactor\n\n| File | State |\n|---|---|\n| `src/assets/partner-logo.svg` | **Kept**, re-authored — vector `<path>` only, viewBox `0 0 80 80`, zero `<image>` elements, `xlink` removed |\n| `src/assets/partner-logo-photo.webp` | **New** — the ex-JPEG, resized and re-encoded |\n| `src/assets/partner-logo-photo.jpg` | **New, optional** — fallback only if your browser matrix needs it |\n\nThe SVG ships throughout — nothing is deleted, and the logo keeps rendering.\n\n## Verifying\n\nRe-run `crimes scan --format json` and confirm `svg_with_embedded_raster` is gone from the findings. Watch two adjacent detectors while you're there: `oversized_raster` (currently firing on `hero-banner.png` at 253.9 KB) will start watching the new `.webp` — keep it under the 200 KB low threshold — and `raster_should_be_vector` fires at ≤64px on both sides, so if the photo ends up small enough to trip it, that's a signal it was icon-sized geometry all along and belonged in group 1.",
